window.docContents[221].resolve({"/Foundations/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Operators___-solution-concepts___-and-weak-formulations--Classical___-weak___-and-well-posed-problems":{"contents":"\n\nThe routes in the figure share these operators, but require different evidence\nabout a proposed solution. A classical calculation supplies pointwise\nidentities; a weak formulation pairs the function with test functions; an\nexistence proof constructs an object satisfying the chosen formulation.\nIntegration by parts relates classical and weak formulations; regularity\ncan recover classical derivatives from a weak solution.\n\nWell-posedness asks a further question about a whole family of problems.\nFor each allowed datum, does a solution exist, is it unique in the chosen\nclass, and do small changes in the data produce small changes in the solution?\nThe word “small” needs a topology on both spaces. The\nwell-posedness definition is\nintroduced through a Dirichlet problem on an interval, where all three\nrequirements are visible in one calculation. Regularity asks how many\nderivatives that solution has; it is a separate conclusion.\n\nFor differentiable initial data  and constant velocity , transport\nhas the classical solution . The chain rule gives\n and , so\n; substituting  gives the initial datum.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Foundations\u0009Operators, solution concepts, and weak formulations","header":"3.1.1. Classical, weak, and well-posed problems","id":"/Foundations/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Operators___-solution-concepts___-and-weak-formulations--Classical___-weak___-and-well-posed-problems"},"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Transport-and-Classical-Waves--Transport-and-Characteristics":{"contents":"We follow Evans, §2.1. A constant velocity  determines\nthe spacetime direction , and the characteristic through  is\n. Integrating the source along this line gives the\nrepresentation formula; differentiating the formula recovers the equation.\n\n\n\nFor a constant spatial velocity , the transport operator is the\nFréchet derivative in spacetime direction . Its codomain is an\narbitrary real normed space, so the same declaration applies to scalar\nunknowns and systems.\n\nThe abstract directional derivative equals the familiar coordinate operator\n\nThe first identity is vector-valued; the second identifies the scalar spatial\nsum with .\n\nDecompose the spacetime direction  into its time basis vector and\nspatial coordinate directions. Linearity of the Fréchet derivative gives the\ntime partial plus the weighted spatial partials; in the scalar case the\nfinite sum is the Euclidean inner product with the gradient.\n\nThe operator by itself fixes only the equation; the Cauchy problem adds a\nprescribed value on the hyperplane .\n\nThe source-parametric initial-value problem consists of\n on  and . Homogeneous\ntransport is the specialization .\n\nThe initial-value problem is equivalent to two clauses: the transport\nresidual on positive time and the assigned value on .\n\nUnfold the initial-value predicate. Its two fields are precisely the\npositive-time residual equation and the assigned initial value, so projection proves\none direction and pairing the two clauses constructs the converse.\n\nLinearity gives the usual reduction of uniqueness to the homogeneous problem\nwith zero initial value.\n\nFix  and assume  and  are Fréchet differentiable at every\nspacetime point with . The residual is linear on positive time, and\ninitial evaluation respects the same combination:Consequently zero, addition, scalar multiplication, negation, subtraction,\nand arbitrary binary linear combinations preserve the corresponding source\nand assigned initial data. In particular, two sufficiently differentiable\nsolutions with the same source and assigned value have a difference\n satisfying  and .\n\nAt positive-time points, differentiability of the fields justifies applying\nthe Fréchet derivative to their linear combination. Evaluation at the initial\nsurface respects the same combination, giving the corresponding sources and\nassigned values. Subtracting equal data gives the homogeneous zero-trace\nproblem. The zero and negation identities also hold without differentiability.\n\nThe first explicit solution concerns the homogeneous case . The field\n is constant along every line of direction , so its\ntime derivative exactly cancels the advection term.\n\nFor differentiable initial data, translation along  solves the\nhomogeneous specialization of , including\nits initial trace.\n\nThe proof differentiates the affine foot map, applies the chain rule to\n, and cancels the time derivative against the spatial advection.\n\nThe translation  in that solution is the point where the line of\ndirection  through  meets the initial hyperplane. Naming this\nline, with its parametrization by time, reduces the forced problem to an\nordinary differential equation along it.\n\nThe characteristic through  is\n. It joins the initial foot  to\n and has constant spacetime velocity .\n\nAlong , a differentiable profile obeysThe endpoint identities  and\n are part of the statement.\n\nThe affine characteristic has derivative . The chain rule evaluates\n on this direction, which is the transport operator by its coordinate\nidentity. Substituting  and  gives the two endpoints.\n\nFor a solution of the forced problem the chain rule along a characteristic\nreads . Integrating from\n, where the curve sits at the initial foot, to  produces the\nrepresentation formula, which is thus a consequence of the equation rather\nthan a separate ansatz.\n\nLet  satisfy the transport residual and initial-value clauses, and let\n. Assume  is Fréchet differentiable in spacetime at\n for every , including the endpoints, and\n is interval integrable on . Then\n\nThe continuous-source variant obtains these hypotheses from global\nFréchet differentiability of  and continuity of .\n\nAmbient differentiability of  along the closed characteristic segment\ngives continuity of its pullback on . On , the chain rule\nand the residual equation give derivative . Its interval\nintegrability permits the fundamental theorem of calculus; the endpoint\nidentities and assigned initial value then give the formula.\n\nThe right-hand side of the representation depends only on the data , ,\nand , not on the solution  it was derived from. Detaching it gives an\nexplicit spacetime field whose properties can be checked directly: that it\nsolves the problem for differentiable data, and that every sufficiently\nregular solution coincides with it.\n\nThe explicit spacetime function isPulling  back to a characteristic gives\nthe initial value plus the accumulated source, while the coordinate theorem\nseparates the footpoint  from time.\n\nFor differentiable  and a  source, the explicit formula is\ndifferentiable, has initial trace , and satisfies the forced transport\nequation. When the source is only continuous, a variant takes\ndifferentiability of the formula as a hypothesis and reaches the same\nconclusion.\n\nFirst write the formula in the adapted coordinates :\n. Since  is , the integrand\nand its derivative with respect to  are continuous. On a compact\nneighborhood of the parameter and time segment, this derivative is uniformly\nbounded, so the parametric integral rule proves spacetime differentiability.\nAdding differentiable  and composing with the linear coordinate map\npreserves it. Restricted to a characteristic, the formula has derivative equal\nto the source by the fundamental theorem of calculus. The characteristic\nchain rule then identifies that derivative with the transport operator, and\nsetting  gives the assigned initial value.\n\nUniqueness needs only the representation theorem: global differentiability\nlets it be applied along every characteristic at once.\n\nEvery globally differentiable solution with continuous source satisfies, for\n,Consequently, if  and  have the same , , and , then\n throughout the nonnegative-time domain.\n\nThe representation theorem identifies every solution with the explicit\nformula determined by , , and . Two solutions with the same data\nare therefore both equal to that formula and hence equal to each other.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Hyperbolic Equations\u0009Transport and Classical Waves","header":"7.2.1. Transport and Characteristics","id":"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Transport-and-Classical-Waves--Transport-and-Characteristics"},"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--The-analytic-language-of-weak-solutions--Approximation-and-the-scale-of-spaces":{"contents":"We follow Chapter 5 of\n. At finite exponent, mollification gives\nsmooth density. Smooth functions are not norm-dense in , so\nthe density theorems stop at finite exponent. On suitable domains, extension\noperators carry functions out to all of  without losing the\nderivatives covered by the operator, and the trace operator recovers boundary\nvalues as a bounded linear map. Strong convergence from compactness passes\nlower-order terms to a limit, while weak convergence of derivatives and lower\nsemicontinuity control the energy.\n\nThe exponents in the embedding theorems are dictated by scaling. Take\n and\n, and set . Then\n\n\n\nSo the homogeneous inequality  has a\nchance of holding with a scale-independent constant only if the two powers\nmatch. Solving  gives the Sobolev conjugate\n for , the only exponent dilation allows. The\nGagliardo–Nirenberg–Sobolev inequality shows that this exponent is attained.\nPast the threshold, when , membership in  buys a\nHölder-continuous representative on  and on domains with suitable\nextension geometry. The full Sobolev norm is doing the work there:\ngradient integrability by itself neither picks out a representative nor controls\nan additive constant. The dimension  is the dividing line between improved\nintegrability and outright continuity.\n\nEvans's Chapter 5 is the PDE-oriented reference;  is\na detailed reference for Sobolev-space theory. The composition of Sobolev functions\nwith Lipschitz maps follows .\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Sobolev Spaces and Weak Derivatives\u0009The analytic language of weak solutions","header":"4.1.2. Approximation and the scale of spaces","id":"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--The-analytic-language-of-weak-solutions--Approximation-and-the-scale-of-spaces"}});